\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Zebrafish larvae lacking Melatonin production show habituation deficits for the probability of response}  \textbf  {A)} Double mutants for the melatonin-synthesizing enzymes Aanat1 and Aanat2 show a decreased modulation of the probability of response to DF stimuli during habituation training relative to heterozygous and WT sibling controls (day-tested cohort, 4 independent experiments: \emph  {aanat1\textsuperscript  {+/?};aanat2\textsuperscript  {+/?}}, n~=~181; \emph  {aanat1\textsuperscript  {-/-};aanat2\textsuperscript  {+/?}}, n~=~121; \emph  {aanat1\textsuperscript  {+/?};aanat2\textsuperscript  {-/-}}, n~=~70; \emph  {aanat1\textsuperscript  {-/-};aanat2\textsuperscript  {-/-}}, n~=~100). Testing was performed during the circadian day, beginning at ZT4, where ZT0 is the time of lights "on" and ZT12 is the time of lights "off". DF stimuli are delivered at 1-minute intervals, in 4 blocks of 60 stimuli, separated by 1hr of rest (from 0:00-7:00). 1.5 hours later a block of 30 vibration stimuli are delivered at 1-minute intervals (\textbf  {i}). Each dot is the probability of response to one DF. Lines are smoothed in time with a Savitzky–Golay filter (window = 15 stimuli, order = 2).   \textbf  {B-F)} Same as A, but for the kinematic components of the O-bend response: \textbf  {B)} the response latency, \textbf  {C)} the maximum tail-bend amplitude achieved during the movement, \textbf  {D)} the duration of the movement, \textbf  {E)} the displacement of the movement, and \textbf  {F)} the reorientation achieved by the larvae. \textbf  {G)} Double mutants for the melatonin-synthesizing enzymes Aanat1 and Aanat2 show a decreased modulation of the probability of response to DF stimuli during habituation training relative to heterozygous and WT sibling controls (night-tested cohort, shifted light cycle, 4 independent experiments: \emph  {aanat1\textsuperscript  {+/?};aanat2\textsuperscript  {+/?}}, n~=~172; \emph  {aanat1\textsuperscript  {-/-};aanat2\textsuperscript  {+/?}}, n~=~123; \emph  {aanat1\textsuperscript  {+/?};aanat2\textsuperscript  {-/-}}, n~=~93; \emph  {aanat1\textsuperscript  {-/-};aanat2\textsuperscript  {-/-}}, n~=~92).   \textbf  {H-L)} Same as G, but for the kinematic components of the O-bend response: \textbf  {H)} the response latency, \textbf  {I)} the maximum tail-bend amplitude achieved during the movement, \textbf  {J)} the duration of the movement, \textbf  {K)} the displacement of the movement, and \textbf  {L)} the reorientation achieved by the larvae. }}{5}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:2}{{2}{5}{\textbf {Zebrafish larvae lacking Melatonin production show habituation deficits for the probability of response}\\ \textbf {A)} Double mutants for the melatonin-synthesizing enzymes Aanat1 and Aanat2 show a decreased modulation of the probability of response to DF stimuli during habituation training relative to heterozygous and WT sibling controls (day-tested cohort, 4 independent experiments: \emph {aanat1\textsuperscript {+/?};aanat2\textsuperscript {+/?}}, n~=~181; \emph {aanat1\textsuperscript {-/-};aanat2\textsuperscript {+/?}}, n~=~121; \emph {aanat1\textsuperscript {+/?};aanat2\textsuperscript {-/-}}, n~=~70; \emph {aanat1\textsuperscript {-/-};aanat2\textsuperscript {-/-}}, n~=~100). Testing was performed during the circadian day, beginning at ZT4, where ZT0 is the time of lights "on" and ZT12 is the time of lights "off". DF stimuli are delivered at 1-minute intervals, in 4 blocks of 60 stimuli, separated by 1hr of rest (from 0:00-7:00). 1.5 hours later a block of 30 vibration stimuli are delivered at 1-minute intervals (\textbf {i}). Each dot is the probability of response to one DF. Lines are smoothed in time with a Savitzky–Golay filter (window = 15 stimuli, order = 2). \\ \textbf {B-F)} Same as A, but for the kinematic components of the O-bend response: \textbf {B)} the response latency, \textbf {C)} the maximum tail-bend amplitude achieved during the movement, \textbf {D)} the duration of the movement, \textbf {E)} the displacement of the movement, and \textbf {F)} the reorientation achieved by the larvae. \textbf {G)} Double mutants for the melatonin-synthesizing enzymes Aanat1 and Aanat2 show a decreased modulation of the probability of response to DF stimuli during habituation training relative to heterozygous and WT sibling controls (night-tested cohort, shifted light cycle, 4 independent experiments: \emph {aanat1\textsuperscript {+/?};aanat2\textsuperscript {+/?}}, n~=~172; \emph {aanat1\textsuperscript {-/-};aanat2\textsuperscript {+/?}}, n~=~123; \emph {aanat1\textsuperscript {+/?};aanat2\textsuperscript {-/-}}, n~=~93; \emph {aanat1\textsuperscript {-/-};aanat2\textsuperscript {-/-}}, n~=~92). \\ \textbf {H-L)} Same as G, but for the kinematic components of the O-bend response: \textbf {H)} the response latency, \textbf {I)} the maximum tail-bend amplitude achieved during the movement, \textbf {J)} the duration of the movement, \textbf {K)} the displacement of the movement, and \textbf {L)} the reorientation achieved by the larvae}{figure.caption.7}{}}
